{
 "nodes": [
  {
   "nodeID": "331",
   "text": "How big is China's surplus really, why does the IMF miss it, and what policy should follow?",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "question",
   "toulmin": "framing",
   "oper": "framing",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#Q"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "332",
   "text": "The surplus stems from a shortfall in China's domestic demand (European Central Bank view)",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "stated_assumption",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.prior",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#P1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "333",
   "text": "Deflation combined with a rising trade surplus calls for sustained fiscal expansion, not monetary easing",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "stated_assumption",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.prior",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#P2"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "334",
   "text": "External balance is a legitimate policy target; exporting your way out of a slump imposes costs on other countries",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "hidden_assumption",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.prior",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#P3"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "335",
   "text": "Export volumes up 40 percent since end-2019 versus imports up 1 percent; manufactured goods surplus over $2 trillion (about 10.5 percent of GDP); goods surplus $1.2 trillion; car exports over 6 million, heading toward 8 million in 2026; current account surplus 2.2 percent of GDP versus the EU's 2.6 percent; IMF forecast a 2025 surplus of $375 billion, while the actual tops $650 billion through the third quarter",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "evidence_data",
   "toulmin": "grounds",
   "oper": "grounds",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#E1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "336",
   "text": "IMF External Sector Report 2025, October World Economic Outlook and 2024 Article IV report; European Central Bank analysis; Netherlands Bureau for Economic Policy Analysis export volume data; Microsoft annual report",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "evidence_source",
   "toulmin": "grounds",
   "oper": "grounds",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#E2"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "337",
   "text": "Weak domestic demand leaves spare capacity for exports; rate cuts lower yields and weaken the yuan, widening the surplus",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "mechanism",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.mechanism",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#M1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "338",
   "text": "The 2022 balance of payments methodology and the unexplained income deficit each cut about 1 percentage point of GDP from the reported current account surplus",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "mechanism",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.mechanism",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#M2"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "339",
   "text": "Relying only on the reported current account frames China's problem as purely internal, so the IMF prescribes monetary easing",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "mechanism",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.mechanism",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#M3"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "340",
   "text": "China's surplus is massive and growing, yet IMF policy advice effectively lets China export its way out of its housing slump",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "intermediate_claim",
   "toulmin": "claim",
   "oper": "claim.step",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#C1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "341",
   "text": "The IMF should recognize China's external imbalance and call for a fiscal expansion funded by the central government",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "main_claim",
   "toulmin": "claim",
   "oper": "claim.final",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#K"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "342",
   "text": "The IMF's framing; the argument that Europe's surplus is almost as big",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "opposing_view",
   "toulmin": "rebuttal",
   "oper": "rebuttal",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#X"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "343",
   "text": "China's GDP data are too thin to confirm the net export math; the IMF does support pro-consumption structural reforms",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "caveat",
   "toulmin": "qualifier",
   "oper": "qualifier",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#QF"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "344",
   "text": "Ireland distorts European data; IMF forecast misses come from ignoring lagged exchange rate effects and treating the pandemic surge as temporary",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "other_explanation",
   "toulmin": "rebuttal",
   "oper": "rebuttal",
   "localID": "setser_2025-11-12_china-surplus-imf#R1"
  },
